course: dictionary
lesson: 3581
title: Fib and small lies
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: A fib is a small lie, usually about something that is not very important. It is an informal word, so you hear it in everyday talk more than in school writing. «Are you telling fibs?» is a question a grown-up might ask when they think you are not telling the truth.
explain: Fib can be a noun or a verb. As a noun, you can tell a fib or tell a little fib. As a verb, to fib means to tell a small lie, and it changes to fibs, fibbed and fibbing.
explain: Context clues tell you how serious a fib is. «Gently he asks why he told such a fib» shows that the fib needs explaining, but gently and asks suggest it is a small untruth, not a terrible crime.
fact: A fib is a small lie, usually about something not very important.
fact: To fib means to tell a small lie. Its forms are fib, fibs, fibbed and fibbing.
fact: Fib is informal, so it is more common in everyday talk than in formal writing.
fact: Useful phrases are tell a fib, a little fib and fib about something.
fact: A context clue like «gently» or «asks why» can show you that a fib is small and not very serious.
exercise: «Are you telling fibs?» What does fibs mean here? | *Small lies | Pieces of wood | Fizzy drinks | Long stories about fishing
exercise: What does it mean to fib? | To hide under a bed | To shout very loudly | *To tell a small lie | To tell a long true story
exercise: «Gently he asks why he told such a fib.» Find and copy one word that means a small lie. | gently | why | asks | *fib
test: Which phrase means telling a small untrue thing? | Tell a map | Tell a song | *Tell a fib | Tell a joke
test: What is a fib usually about? | The biggest thing in the world | *Something not very important | The weather on a holiday
exam: Which sentence uses fib as a noun? | Please fib less often. | *Are you telling fibs? | Tom wants to fib about his homework. | She fibbed yesterday.
